# Scope canvas — {{PROJECT_NAME}}

A short canvas for Outline section 1, combining the user-centered questions of the [U.S. Digital Services Playbook](https://playbook.usds.gov/) with the partnership questions of the [Data Collaboratives Canvas](https://datacollaboratives.org/canvas.html). Use it to surface the hard parts (who it serves, incentives, risks, terms) early instead of mid-project. Right-size it: a one-team project can answer most blocks in a line; a cross-sector partnership should work through each. An empty or hand-waved block means more discovery is needed before building. Record the decisions it produces in `decision-log.md`; the agreed terms feed `docs/governance/CHARTER.md` and `docs/governance/GOVERNANCE.md`.

## People and needs

Name the design partners (who does the work) and the beneficiaries (who it is for) separately, rather than a generic "the public." For each, state the need in their words and how you know it (interviews, observation, data), not how you assume it.

## Problem and why now

The problem from the beneficiaries' point of view, what makes it worth solving now, and the value each partner gets that it could not get alone. Keep it legible to someone with none of the project's context; it becomes the README's "What & why."

## Data supply and gaps

The data that exists across the partners, in what form and condition, and what is missing or would have to be collected, requested (FOIA) or acquired to answer the question.

## Partners and incentives

Each partner and the motivation that brings it to the table. Name the asymmetries (a large agency and a small community group) and how you will keep the playing field level.

## Model of exchange

How data moves: pooling, a research partnership, an API or query interface, a trusted intermediary, zero-copy sharing, a public release. Say why that model fits the data's sensitivity and the project's visibility (private, embargoed or public).

## Risks and mitigation

For each stage, name the foreseeable risks, their rough likelihood and severity, and the mitigation and monitoring. For sensitive data, the responsible-data checklist's findings are inputs here.

- **Collection:** consent, provenance and minimization.
- **Analysis:** re-identification, spurious inference, biased methods.
- **Sharing:** exposure while moving data between partners.
- **Use:** misuse, profiling or downstream harm once results are released.

## Terms

Who bears liability, how intellectual property in inputs and outputs is handled, who owns what (data, derivatives, code), and what will be released, when, and under which licenses (the README's License section).

## Governance and roles

How decisions are made and contested, who can halt or redirect the work, and who does what (map to `docs/governance/CHARTER.md`). Couple every retention, identifier or audit capability to its access tier and remedy.

## Outcomes and how you will know

The change you intend for the beneficiaries, the baseline you measure against, and the signals that would show it is working. Prefer outcome measures (was the need met?) over output measures (did we ship?).

## Smallest useful version

The smallest thing you can put in front of real users to learn whether you are on the right track, what you will measure, and how feedback changes the next iteration.

## Communication

The cadence and channels that keep partners and beneficiaries informed, who speaks for the collaboration, and how findings reach non-expert audiences accessibly.
